## Firewall (UFW on DO rpc-1)

```
22/tcp                ALLOW    Anywhere      # SSH
8545,8595,8547/tcp    ALLOW    92.243.26.189 # BLUE rope-vps
8545,8595,8547/tcp    ALLOW    92.243.25.119 # GREEN anvil-vps
8545,8595,8547/tcp    ALLOW    92.243.26.114 # dcswap-vps
8545,8595,8547/tcp    DENY     Anywhere      # public denied
9000,30304            ALLOW    Anywhere      # libp2p + Reth p2p
```

Public internet **cannot** reach DO RPC ports — only the Gandi infrastructure (which hosts `erpc.datachain.network` nginx) can reach DO.

## State-sync mechanism

Two scripts on BLUE in `/opt/datachain-rope/scripts/`:

### `reth-do-sync.sh` — routine (cron every 15 min)

Hot rsync from BLUE → DO. **BLUE never stops.** DO Reth is briefly stopped for the rsync window (~30-60 s) to avoid mdbx concurrent-write corruption on destination. Then verifies block height + deployer nonce match.

Cron: `7,22,37,52 * * * *` (offset 7 min from `reth-blue-green-sync.sh` to spread load).

### `reth-do-bootstrap.sh` — recovery (manual)

Used only when DO mdbx is corrupt or empty. Briefly stops BLUE (~3-4 min), streams an uncompressed tar of the data dir over SSH, restarts both. GREEN takes over public traffic during BLUE's stop window.

**Why two scripts?** Empirical finding (2026-05-03): hot rsync over a *clean* DO data dir works (mdbx tolerates small page-level deltas). Hot rsync of a *full bootstrap* (6.2 GB cold) corrupts mdbx because the long transfer window encompasses many BLUE writes. The split keeps routine cycles cheap (zero BLUE downtime) while having a proven cold-start path.

